fclose drops what it could not write, as glibc and musl do, and still
returns EOF. When the flush failed, as a write to a pipe whose reader has
gone does (EPIPE), the bytes stayed in the buffer, and the close and the
stream's end printed "mlibc warning: File is not flushed before closing"
and "... before destruction" on the program's standard error. PHP's
mail() writes the message to the command of sendmail_path through popen,
and a command that ends without reading it (PHP's tests mail/gh10990 and
mail/gh20257) left those lines in the program's output. Meant for
upstream.

diff --git a/options/ansi/generic/file-io.cpp b/options/ansi/generic/file-io.cpp
index 744870e..2cdf8e7 100644
--- a/options/ansi/generic/file-io.cpp
+++ b/options/ansi/generic/file-io.cpp
@@ -764,8 +764,11 @@ FILE *fopen(const char *path, const char *mode) {
 int fclose(FILE *file_base) {
 	auto file = static_cast<mlibc::abstract_file *>(file_base);
 	int e = 0;
-	if(file->flush())
+	// What could not be written is dropped, as glibc and musl drop it.
+	if(file->flush()) {
+		file->purge();
 		e = EOF;
+	}
 	if(file->close())
 		e = EOF;
 	file->dispose();
